#
# D-132 — the ESCALATED class, rendered rather than asserted. Five nested
# manifest shapes driven through BOTH spawn doors, ten cells.
#
# THE SAME TEN CELLS BEFORE THE FIX, driven through server.py's _DISPATCH:
#
#   castings="nope"          Spawn-Teammate RAISES AttributeError 'str'
#                            Cast-Wave      tolerated
#   castings=[1,2,3]         Spawn-Teammate RAISES AttributeError 'int'
#                            Cast-Wave      tolerated
#   castings=[null]          Spawn-Teammate RAISES AttributeError 'NoneType'
#                            Cast-Wave      tolerated
#   waves="nope"             Spawn-Teammate tolerated
#                            Cast-Wave      RAISES AttributeError 'str'
#   castings=[{"no_id":1}]   both tolerated
#   TOTAL 4/10 RAISE, 0/10 name manifest.json
#
# D-115 guarded the manifest's top-level CONTAINER and stopped, so the RECORDS
# inside it that every reader then indexes were never guarded. An AttributeError
# reaching call_tool becomes a raw traceback, and this module's whole error
# contract is a named {"ok": False, "error", "hint"} refusal (NFR-002 / AC-025).
#
# THE ASYMMETRY IS THE TELL (same as D-097): the two doors disagreed about the
# same corrupt document. The tolerated half is arguably the worse half — a
# structurally corrupt manifest was reported as a MISSING WAVE or a MISSING
# CASTING, which sends the operator to rebuild wave groupings in a file whose
# real problem is one rung above them.
#
# The fix is not five filters at five index sites; that is the escalated class
# itself. It is ONE declaration (_MANIFEST_SHAPE) walked by ONE recursive
# validator (_shape_problem) that all four manifest readers in the module
# consult — so the two doors produce byte-identical text BY CONSTRUCTION rather
# than by two hand-written guards agreeing with each other. The last column of
# every row below is one string printed once because both doors returned it.
#
# The run directory is elided as <run> by the test so the render is byte-stable
# under re-execution; the assertions in test_both_doors_refuse_a_nested_shape_
# with_identical_text run against the real absolute path the operator receives.
#

